<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Doc 36 § F7 wave 6. The ledger's one **live**-model row: "port; signal-back
    `RemoteInspectorClient` additively, per `DeviceManager`".

    ⚠ **Additive means the line is drawn between what a binding can express and what it cannot, and
    this panel has both halves in it.** `TreeView` is painted from `TreeNode` *data* by `Refresh()` —
    the panel ledger's shape 1, named there in as many words — so the entity tree stays imperative
    and stays fed by `RemoteInspectorClient.Changed`, exactly as `DeviceManagerView`'s `DataGrid`
    does. What the signals buy is the rest of `Restate`: the sentence, the Attach button's label, and
    the three `Disabled` flags.

    ⚠ **`Restate` is gone rather than moved, and it was doing five unrelated things once a frame.**
    `RemoteInspectorClient.Poll` runs from the panel's tick, so every path that raised `Changed`
    re-labelled a button, re-wrote a sentence, rebuilt the whole entity tree and re-ran the counter
    pool — whether or not anything had changed. Four of the five are bindings now, each with its own
    dependency, and an unchanged poll writes no signal and runs no effect at all.

    ⚠ **The counter pane's pool is the second thing deleted.** `Counters` kept a `List<UiElement>`
    that only ever grew and parked the surplus with a class; a keyed `@for` over a sorted projection
    is the same list with nothing left over. Sorted for the reason the C# gave — a dictionary's order
    is its hashing, and a pane of live numbers that reordered itself as values arrived would be
    unreadable — and keyed on the whole row, because a changed reading must be a changed identity.

    ⚠ **Three `Disabled` flags, and `UiTest.Tree` cannot see one of them.** Wave 5 found that; this
    panel's correctness is almost entirely in those flags, so the harness dumps
    `state=`/`disabled=` as an extra line per element in all nine states rather than trusting the
    rectangles.

    ⚠ **`Selected` is the view's own signal and not the tree's, and it is fed by
    `change:SelectedNodes`.** Wave 6 tried `change:Selection` and got "'tree-view' has no property
    called 'Selection'" at compose, and concluded that a selection could not be a `[UiProperty]` at
    all. Half of that was right: `Selection` is a `HashSet` behind a read-only view, the same
    instance before and after every change, so nothing riding `PropertyChanged` could ever report it.
    What was missing was a *value*, which `TreeView.SelectedNodes` now is — so the `ref` plus
    `OnComposed` subscription this panel used to need is one attribute, and one that fires strictly
    less often than `SelectionChanged` did: a click on the row already selected is not a change.
-->
@component RemoteInspectorView
@namespace Vixen.Editor.Debugger
@inherits Vixen.Ui.Controls.Control
@tag remote-inspector
@using System.Collections.Immutable
@using System.Globalization
@using Vixen.Ui
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced
@using Vixen.Ui.Reactive

<remote-toolbar ref="@Toolbar">
    <Button ref="@AttachButton"
            Size="Small"
            Variant="Primary"
            Label="@AttachLabel"
            on:click="@Toggle" />

    <Button ref="@RefreshButton"
            Size="Small"
            Variant="Subtle"
            Label="Refresh"
            Disabled="@(!IsAttached)"
            on:click="@(() => Client?.Refresh())" />
</remote-toolbar>

<RemoteStatus ref="@Status" Text="@Sentence()" />

<remote-body>
    <!--
        ⚠ A `ref` for the rows and an attribute for the selection, which is the ledger's shape 1 with
        one half of it now closed. A `TreeView`'s rows are `TreeNode` data painted by `Refresh()`;
        there is no element for markup to describe and no attribute that could bind one, so
        `Entities()` below is the hand-written method unchanged. The *selection* is a value, and
        `change:` names values.
    -->
    <TreeView ref="@Tree" change:SelectedNodes="@(_ => Chose())" />

    <remote-counters ref="@CounterPane">
        @for (var counter in CounterRows) {
            <remote-counter key="@counter">
                <CounterLabel Text="@counter.Name" />
                <CounterValue Text="@counter.Reading" />
            </remote-counter>
        }
    </remote-counters>
</remote-body>

<remote-write>
    <TextBox ref="@Member" Placeholder="Component.Member" Size="Small" change:Value="@(_ => Typed())" />
    <TextBox ref="@Value" Placeholder="Value" Size="Small" />

    <Button ref="@Apply" Size="Small" Label="Set" Disabled="@(!CanWrite)" on:click="@Write" />
</remote-write>

@code {
    /// <summary>The client, as a signal so that <see cref="Show" /> re-reads the whole panel.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>A signal over the client <i>as well as</i> signals inside it, and both are load
    ///     bearing.</b> `DeviceManagerView` says the same thing about its manager: this one says a
    ///     different client arrived, the ones on <see cref="RemoteInspectorClient" /> say the same
    ///     client's connection moved. A panel holding a plain field here would say "No connection
    ///     configured." for ever.
    /// </remarks>
    readonly Signal<RemoteInspectorClient?> connection = new(null);

    /// <summary>Which entity the tree has selected.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Still the view's own signal, and now for the remaining reason rather than the old
    ///     one.</b> <c>TreeView.SelectedNodes</c> is a property and <c>change:</c> reaches it — but
    ///     what the <c>Disabled</c> bindings need is the <see cref="RemoteEntity" /> hanging off the
    ///     first selected node's <c>Tag</c>, which is this panel's projection and not the control's.
    ///     See the file header.
    /// </remarks>
    readonly Signal<RemoteEntity?> chosen = new(null);

    /// <summary>What is typed in the member box, which Apply's greying depends on.</summary>
    readonly Signal<string> member = new(string.Empty);

    Action<RemoteInspectorClient>? onChanged;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override bool AcceptsFocus => false;

    /// <summary>The strip along the top.</summary>
    public UiElement Toolbar { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Attaches and detaches.</summary>
    public Button AttachButton { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Asks for the tree again.</summary>
    public Button RefreshButton { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>What the connection is doing.</summary>
    public UiElement Status { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The entities.</summary>
    public TreeView Tree { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The live numbers.</summary>
    public UiElement CounterPane { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Which member to write, as <c>Component.Member</c>.</summary>
    public TextBox Member { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>What to write into it.</summary>
    public TextBox Value { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Writes it.</summary>
    public Button Apply { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Which entity is selected, or <see langword="null" />.</summary>
    public RemoteEntity? Selected => chosen.Value;

    /// <summary>Points the panel at a client.</summary>
    /// <param name="inspector">The client.</param>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentNullException"><paramref name="inspector" /> is null.</exception>
    public void Show(RemoteInspectorClient inspector) {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(inspector);

        Release();

        connection.Value = inspector;
        onChanged ??= _ => Entities();

        inspector.Changed += onChanged;
        Entities();
    }

    /// <summary>Advances the connection.</summary>
    /// <param name="elapsed">How much time has passed.</param>
    public void Tick(TimeSpan elapsed) => connection.Value?.Poll(elapsed);

    /// <summary>
    ///     ⚠ <c>OnUnmounted</c> and not an <c>OnRemoved</c> override, because the generated
    ///     element-flavoured scaffold already has one — it is where the composition is stopped. This
    ///     is the hook it calls, and it is the same place <c>AudioMixerView</c> lets go of its
    ///     document.
    /// </summary>
    partial void OnUnmounted() => Release();

    /// <summary>Rebuilds the entity tree from the far end's flat list.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     <para>
    ///         ⚠ <b>Off <see cref="RemoteInspectorClient.Changed" /> and off nothing else, which is
    ///         the same split <c>DeviceManagerView.Fill</c> makes.</b> A <c>TreeView</c> is fed by
    ///         methods over <c>TreeNode</c> data, so this half could never have been a binding; what
    ///         it gains from the port is that it no longer runs when only a counter moved.
    ///     </para>
    ///     <para>
    ///         ⚠ <b>Parents are resolved by id, and an entity whose parent has not arrived is a
    ///         root.</b> The far end sends its entities in whatever order it walks them, and a build
    ///         streaming a large scene may not send a parent before its child — so a rebuild that
    ///         waited for the parent would drop half the tree.
    ///     </para>
    /// </remarks>
    void Entities() {
        while (Tree.Root.Children.Count > 0) {
            Tree.Root.Remove(Tree.Root.Children[^1]);
        }

        chosen.Value = null;

        if (connection.Value is not { } inspector) {
            Tree.Refresh();
            return;
        }

        Dictionary<ulong, TreeNode> byId = [];

        foreach (var entity in inspector.Entities) {
            byId[entity.Id] = new(Label(entity), entity);
        }

        foreach (var entity in inspector.Entities) {
            var node = byId[entity.Id];

            if (entity.Parent != 0 && byId.TryGetValue(entity.Parent, out var parent)) {
                parent.Add(node);
            } else {
                Tree.Root.Add(node);
            }
        }

        foreach (var node in byId.Values) {
            if (node.HasChildren) {
                Tree.Expand(node);
            }
        }

        Tree.Refresh();
    }

    void Release() {
        if (connection.Value is { } inspector && onChanged is not null) {
            inspector.Changed -= onChanged;
        }
    }

    void Toggle() {
        if (connection.Value is not { } inspector) {
            return;
        }

        if (inspector.State is RemoteState.Detached) {
            inspector.Attach();
        } else {
            inspector.Detach();
        }
    }

    void Chose() {
        RemoteEntity? found = null;

        foreach (var node in Tree.Selection) {
            if (node.Tag is RemoteEntity entity) {
                found = entity;
                break;
            }
        }

        chosen.Value = found;
    }

    void Typed() => member.Value = Member.Value ?? string.Empty;

    void Write() {
        if (connection.Value is { } inspector && Selected is { } entity && member.Value is { Length: > 0 } name) {
            inspector.SetValue(entity.Id, name, Value.Value ?? string.Empty);
        }
    }

    static string Label(RemoteEntity entity) =>
        entity.Components.Count == 0
            ? entity.Name
            : entity.Name + "  ·  " + string.Join(", ", entity.Components);

    // ================================================================== What the markup reads

    /// <summary>The client, as the bindings read it.</summary>
    RemoteInspectorClient? Client => connection.Value;

    bool IsAttached => connection.Value?.State is RemoteState.Attached;

    string AttachLabel => connection.Value?.State is RemoteState.Detached ? "Attach" : "Detach";

    /// <summary>Whether there is a selection and a member name to write it into.</summary>
    bool CanWrite => IsAttached && chosen.Value is not null && member.Value.Length > 0;

    /// <summary>What the panel has to say about the connection.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Counting <c>Entities</c> here is what subscribes to it.</b> It is a
    ///     <c>CollectionSignal</c>, so reading its count inside this binding is the dependency that
    ///     makes the sentence follow a tree arriving — no <c>Restate</c>, and no notification the
    ///     client has to remember to raise.
    /// </remarks>
    string Sentence() {
        if (connection.Value is not { } inspector) {
            return "No connection configured.";
        }

        return inspector.State switch {
            RemoteState.Detached => "Detached.",
            RemoteState.Attaching => "Attaching…",
            RemoteState.Incompatible => string.Create(
                CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                $"'{inspector.BuildName}' speaks protocol {inspector.BuildVersion}; this editor speaks "
                + $"{InspectorProtocol.Version}."
            ),
            _ => string.Create(
                CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                $"Attached to {inspector.BuildName} — {inspector.Entities.Count:N0} entit(ies){(inspector.IsFetching ? ", fetching…" : "")}"
            )
        };
    }

    /// <summary>One row per counter, by name.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Sorted, and the C#'s comment is the reason: a dictionary's order is its hashing, and
    ///     a pane of live numbers that reordered itself as values arrived would be unreadable.</b>
    ///     ⚠ <b>And keyed on the whole record</b>, which is the immutable-data half of the
    ///     <c>@for</c> rule — a counter that moves from 59.5 to 61.25 must be a different key, or the
    ///     row would go on showing the first reading.
    /// </remarks>
    ImmutableArray<CounterRow> CounterRows =>
        connection.Value is not { } inspector
            ? []
            : [
                .. inspector.Counters.Keys.Order(StringComparer.Ordinal)
                    .Select(name => new CounterRow(
                        name,
                        inspector.Counters[name].ToString("0.###", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                    ))
            ];
}
